<LegalPage
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="What extport collects, why, who processes it, and how long it is kept."
  updated="November 18, 2026"
>
  <p class="lead">
    extport is a service for browser-extension developers: it publishes their extensions to the browser stores,
    reports usage analytics, and issues and verifies license keys for the extensions they sell. This policy explains
    what data extport handles in doing that. It is written for three groups of people:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Developers</strong> who sign in to extport to manage their extensions.</li>
    <li><strong>Buyers</strong> who purchased a license for an extension that uses extport licensing.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Extension users</strong> who installed an extension whose developer turned on extport analytics.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    extport does not sell personal data, does not show ads, and does not use third-party tracking or advertising
    cookies on extport.dev, dash.extport.dev, or portal.extport.dev.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is responsible for your data</h2>
  <p>
    For developer accounts, extport decides how that data is used and is responsible for it. For buyers and extension
    users, the extension's developer is the one you have a relationship with: they sell the extension and decide to
    use extport for licensing or analytics. extport processes that data on their behalf and only to provide those
    features to them. If you have a question about an extension, contact its developer first. The buyer portal shows a
    help link for each purchase when the developer has set one. You can always contact extport directly as well.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>

  <h3>Developers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Account details from GitHub</strong> when you sign in: your GitHub user ID, email address, and display
      name.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Store credentials and payment secrets</strong> you enter (for example, browser store API keys and your
      Stripe webhook signing secret). These are encrypted with a key unique to your account before being stored, and
      the dashboard never shows them again in full.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Extension packages</strong> you upload for publishing, and the publishing history for each store.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>API keys and sessions:</strong> only a hash of each API key and session token is stored, never the
      token itself.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Settings</strong> you configure, such as the support link shown to your buyers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Buyers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Purchase details</strong> sent by the developer's Stripe account when you complete a checkout: your
      email address and the Stripe payment references needed to handle refunds. extport never sees or stores your card
      or other payment details. Payment happens entirely in the developer's Stripe account.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Your license key</strong>, its status, and the plan it belongs to.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Device activations:</strong> when you activate a license, the extension sends an identifier it
      generates for that installation. extport stores that identifier, the time of activation, the time the device was
      last seen, and the IP address and browser user agent of the activation request. These are used to enforce the
      license's device limit and to help the developer investigate abuse.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Buyer portal sign-in:</strong> a one-time sign-in link sent to your email, and a session cookie once you
      sign in.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Extension users (analytics)</h3>
  <p>
    If a developer turns on extport analytics, the extension sends one request a day containing a random installation
    ID (not linked to your identity, your license, or your license activations), the extension's version, and your
    browser's language. From that request extport also derives your browser and operating system from the user agent,
    and your country from the request's IP address. <strong>The IP address itself is not stored.</strong> Dates are
    recorded at day granularity. No browsing activity or page content is collected.
  </p>

  <h3>Everyone</h3>
  <p>
    The service runs on Cloudflare, which processes standard request data (such as IP addresses) to deliver and
    protect the service. extport sets only the cookies needed to keep you signed in.
  </p>

  <h2>How we use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>To provide the service: publishing, analytics, license fulfillment, and activation checks.</li>
    <li>
      To send service emails: sign-in links, activation codes, and publishing notifications. We do not send marketing
      email.
    </li>
    <li>To keep the service secure and prevent abuse.</li>
    <li>To respond when you contact us.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Service providers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare</strong>: hosting, database, file storage, queues, analytics storage, and email delivery.
    </li>
    <li><strong>GitHub</strong>: developer sign-in.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stripe</strong>: the developer's own Stripe account sends purchase and refund events to extport. extport
      has no access to that account beyond verifying those events.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Browser stores</strong> (Google Chrome Web Store, Mozilla Add-ons, Microsoft Edge Add-ons, Apple App
      Store Connect): extport calls their APIs with the developer's own credentials to publish and check review status.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>We do not share personal data with anyone else except where required by law.</p>

  <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Developer account data: while the account exists. Ask us to delete your account at any time.</li>
    <li>
      License records: for as long as the developer's account keeps them, since a license is the buyer's proof of
      purchase.
    </li>
    <li>Sign-in links expire after 15 minutes. Sessions expire automatically.</li>
    <li>
      Raw daily analytics requests: up to 90 days. After that only aggregate counts (for example, daily active users
      per version or country) and a per-installation record of first and last day seen remain.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask us to access, correct, export, or delete your personal data by emailing
    <a href="mailto:support@extport.dev">support@extport.dev</a>. If the request concerns data a developer controls
    (for example, your license for their extension), we may forward it to that developer or ask you to contact them,
    since some records, like proof of purchase, are theirs to keep. Extension users can stop analytics by uninstalling
    the extension.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>extport is not directed at children under 13 and we do not knowingly collect their data.</p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If we change this policy, we will update the date at the top of this page. For significant changes we will also
    notify developers by email or in the dashboard.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about this policy or your data: <a href="mailto:support@extport.dev">support@extport.dev</a>.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
